Curiosity is a vital intrinsic motivation that fuels self-driven learning and the ability to acquire new knowledge by linking new information to one's existing knowledge base. Due to its abstract nature and varying manifestations, accurately measuring curiosity remains a significant challenge. Furthermore, the fluctuating nature of curiosity within individuals during learning processes presents another challenge. To tackle these challenges, we propose a innovative approach that utilizes a combination of visual and auditory data to gauge the curiosity levels of learners within learning groups. Our approach leverages the power of multimodal analysis to extract meaningful cues from visual and auditory data, which are then processed by a neural network to predict the curiosity levels of participants. Our approach was tested across three different learning scenarios that involved nine small learning groups, which were observed using video recordings. Our results demonstrate the effectiveness of our approach, which achieved an accuracy of 71% in predicting the curiosity levels of participants. Our approach also highlights the importance of visual and auditory data in the context of curiosity measurement, which may have broader implications for the design of effective learning environments.

